  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  High pressure bringing stable conditions through Wednesday.\n\n-  Light precipition is possible Thursday onward.\n\nHigh pressure has settled over the region with strong valley\ninversions developing. The current temperature difference between\nPoint 6, Snow Bowl Ski area, and the airport in Missoula is 10\ndegrees. Its a balmy 44F at 8000 feet, while just 34F in the\nvalley. These inversions will suppress maximum warming potential\nin the valleys today and tomorrow and allow for valley fog and \nlow stratus to linger into the afternoon. \n\nStarting Thursday, while the region generally remains under the\neffects of high pressure, weak systems will start to dive south.\nEach of these systems will be capable of light precipitation\nmainly in the mountains. \n\nSaturday one of these systems may be strong enough to temporarily\nsuppress the ridge and southwesterly breezes develop. \n\nBeyond a breezy afternoon Saturday and light precipitation in the\nmountains the Northern Rockies remains in between systems and no\nwidespread precipitation events are expected through at least 11 \nNov.",
